Description: face authentication integrated with the Ubuntu login system
 Sign in, unlock the screen, use sudo or answer administrator prompts with
 your face, through PAM.  The password stays available at every step: while
 the camera looks for your face you can type your password at any moment, and
 a failed scan never counts as a failed login nor locks the account.
 .
 A PAM module (pam_facial_auth.so) asks an unprivileged, sandboxed daemon
 (facial-authd) that matches faces with the OpenCV YuNet and SFace models.
 Faces are linked to accounts by an administrator (polkit); only embeddings
 are stored, never photographs.
 .
 A colour webcam can be fooled by a photograph, so privilege escalation (sudo,
 su, polkit) requires an infrared camera unless the administrator opts in.

Package: facial-auth-gtk
Architecture: all
Depends:
 facial-auth (= ${source:Version}),
 gir1.2-adw-1,
 gir1.2-gdkpixbuf-2.0,
 gir1.2-gtk-4.0,
 python3-gi,
 python3-gi-cairo,
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${python3:Depends},
Description: GTK settings application for facial-auth
 "Face Login": preview the camera or import photos taken with the Camera
 application, then let an administrator link the face to an account.  Also
 lets users test, disable or remove their faces and choose where the face may
 be used.
